course: dictionary
lesson: 5076
title: Not enough: the word insufficient
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Insufficient means not enough. Something is insufficient when it is less than what is needed. If you need ten stickers for a game and you only have four, your stickers are insufficient.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «Insufficient funds» means there is not enough money to pay for something. «Insufficient evidence» means there are not enough facts to prove something. «Insufficient time» means there is not enough time to finish.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in. Put not enough where insufficient was. If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Insufficient is a formal word, so you will meet it in exams, official letters and messages more than in chatty talk.
fact: Insufficient means not enough, or less than what is needed.
fact: «Insufficient funds» means not enough money to pay for something.
fact: With £3 in the account, a £4.50 snack is £4.50 − £3 = £1.50 short, so the funds are insufficient.
fact: Insufficient is a formal word, used in exams, official letters and messages. In everyday speech, people often say not enough instead.
fact: The opposite of insufficient is sufficient, which means enough.
exercise: Ben says, «We have insufficient time to finish the test.» What does insufficient mean here? | *Not enough | Very long | Very fast | Plenty to spare
exercise: «The police had insufficient evidence to charge him.» What does insufficient evidence mean? | Evidence found under the sea | *Not enough facts to prove it | A big pile of clues that proves it | Evidence that is very old
exercise: The shop says the card has insufficient funds. What does this mean? | The card is new and shiny | *There is not enough money in the account | The shop is closed today | The card has a picture on it
test: Which word means the opposite of insufficient? | Scarce | Short | Limited | *Sufficient
test: Dani writes, «The report had insufficient detail.» Which change keeps the same meaning? | The report had far too much detail | The report had detail in red | The report had enough detail | *The report had not enough detail
exam: Which sentence uses insufficient correctly to mean not enough? | The dog was insufficient and very loud | The cake was insufficient, so we ate five slices | The insufficient bus was bright blue | *We had insufficient flour, so we could not bake the cake
